 ## Overview
-Photometric Reconstruction Loss minimizes the error between rendered pixel colors and input training images during joint optimization of scene representation and camera parameters [S3, unofficial]. It distinguishes itself from standard pixel-wise losses by explicitly mapping 3D sample coordinates from a canonical ray to camera-specific rays via rigid transformations, ensuring geometric consistency across views [S3, unofficial]. Unlike sibling methods that may rely solely on intensity matching without explicit 3D warping, this method enforces that the radiance field evaluated at the transformed 3D position matches the observed image color [S3, unofficial].
+Photometric Reconstruction Loss minimizes the error between rendered pixel colors and input training images during joint optimization of scene representation and camera parameters [S3, unofficial]. It distinguishes itself from standard pixel-wise losses by explicitly mapping 3D sample coordinates from a canonical ray to camera-specific rays via rigid transformations, ensuring geometric consistency across views [S3, unofficial]. Unlike sibling methods such as [Color Volume Optimization and Density Initialization](842-color-volume-optimization-and-density-initialization.md) or [Regularization losses](1413-regularization-losses.md) that may rely solely on intensity matching or explicit 3D constraints, this method enforces that the radiance field evaluated at the transformed 3D position matches the observed image color [S3, unofficial]. It builds on the differentiability of the forward rendering operator and gradient propagation through integration steps described in [Optimization Procedures](7150-optimization-procedures.md) [44-radiance-field-optimization.md].
 
